  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none",
    "relation": "tests",
    "tranche": "T3",
    "tranche_reason": "Tests unlearning outcomes in multimodal models, extending the question to visual concepts in MLLMs. It does not test post-unlearning recovery attacks.",
    "gate_note": "The paper evaluates visual-question accuracy after unlearning and compares it with the vanilla model. It reports no fine-tuning recovery attack, activation-direction ablation, quantization attack, or prompt-only recovery test. The paper describes random guessing as the ideal, but does not state a quantified chance level. Cited findings and parameter-distribution visualizations are not separate behavioral outcomes for the claim.",
    "test_reason": "A new setting: visual-concept unlearning in 7B and 13B multimodal models. It reports forget-set VQA accuracy and utility after unlearning, comparing four LLM unlearning methods with a new saliency-masked method. This bears only on the first half of A (removal with ability kept). No recovery attack of any kind is run, so B–F and A's attack-resistance clause are untested.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly correct. Authors conclude that transferring LLM unlearning methods to VQA data is insufficient to erase visual concepts, and that MMUnlearner removes them while preserving textual and untargeted knowledge. They also treat CLEAR results as references, not decisive evidence, and acknowledge residual utility loss.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "Adapts GA, GA_Diff, KL_Min, and NPO to multimodal VQA unlearning and proposes a geometry-constrained, saliency-mask-based method. It does not implement the recovery attacks in the claim.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The paper reports that CLEAR results can be unpredictable and that minor hyperparameter changes sometimes caused model collapse; it also reports different outcomes across benchmarks, models, and forget ratios.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Baselines trained on VQA forget data mostly lower text-only QA accuracy about the forget entities, rather than visual recognition. Example: LLaVA MLLMU Forget QA 38.4→32.5 under GA.",
        "proposed_claim": "In multimodal models, LLM unlearning methods applied to VQA data erase the textual knowledge rather than the visual concept they target.",
        "row_ids": []
      }
    ],
    "controls": "y",
    "capability_check": "y",
    "uncertainty_reported": "n",
    "code_released": "not stated",
    "peer_reviewed": "Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics",
    "problems": "No recovery attacks are tested. The paper does not provide a quantified chance level for the Forget VQA accuracy. The authors note that CLEAR results are unstable and should not be decisive evidence; model utility also degrades after unlearning.",
    "rows_left_out": "Other Table 1 metrics, including textual QA, retained-set and real-world performance, plus the forget-ratio plots, are not separately extracted here. They address preservation and utility but do not test recovery attacks; the study's stated outcomes on these dimensions are summarized in authors_conclusion and capability_check.",
    "untested": [
      {
        "version": "A",
        "parts": "Resistance to simple attacks; whether forget accuracy reaches chance (chance level never quantified)."
      },
      {
        "version": "B",
        "parts": "All parts: no relearning or fine-tuning attack."
      },
      {
        "version": "C",
        "parts": "All parts: no activation ablation. The parameter heatmaps are not a recovery test."
      },
      {
        "version": "D",
        "parts": "All parts: no quantization attack."
      },
      {
        "version": "E",
        "parts": "All parts: no prompting, rephrasing or jailbreak recovery."
      },
      {
        "version": "F",
        "parts": "MMUnlearner is not claimed or tested to resist any recovery attack."
      }
    ],
    "missing": [
      {
        "what": "LLaVA-1.5-13B Forget VQA accuracy: vanilla 52.5%, MMUnlearner 30.0%, GA 40.0%, GA_Diff 40.8%, KL_Min 39.2%, NPO 32.8%; plus utility columns.",
        "locator": "Appendix C.1, Table 6",
        "versions": "A"
      },
      {
        "what": "Module ablation for MMUnlearner on LLaVA-7B / MLLMU-Bench. Forget VQA: LM+Connector 30.4%, Vision Encoder only 33.6%. Updating the vision encoder alone cuts Realworld VQA to 38.3%.",
        "locator": "Section 4.4, Table 2",
        "versions": "A"
      }
    ],
    "verdict_notes": "No recovery attack, so the paper cannot speak to whether the forgetting is shallow. A verdict was given only for the method-level removal half of A. The paper says the ideal is random-guessing behaviour but never states a chance accuracy, so the near-chance check stays open; MMUnlearner rows are therefore cannot_determine rather than supports. The authors call CLEAR runs unstable, with minor hyperparameter changes sometimes collapsing the model. The proposal is the authors' own method, and baseline comparisons use shared hyperparameters."
  },
